.TH CCOL_FOR_EACH 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_for_each \- iterate over all elements of any supported container
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/citerators.h>   /* or any container header */

ccol_for_each(container, it, { body });
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
ccol_for_each declares a cmap_iterator* named it with RAII cleanup, then
walks from ccol_begin(container) to NULL with ccol_iter_next() and runs
the body once for each element. Inside the body you get typed access to
the element through ccol_iter_key_ptr(it) and ccol_iter_val_ptr(it).

The iterator is destroyed when the loop ends, whether the loop runs to
the end of the sequence or leaves through a break or a return.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
container  A cvec, chmap or cbmap variable.
it         Name for the iterator variable.
body       Compound statement to run for each element. The macro takes
           it as a variadic argument, so a comma inside a nested function
           call is safe.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Do NOT change the container inside the body. An insertion or a deletion
invalidates the iterator and the behavior is undefined.
The macro uses statement expressions and the cleanup attribute, so it
needs GCC or Clang.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
chmap_construct(freq, char*, int);
/* ... fill the map ... */
ccol_for_each(freq, it, {
    printf("%-12s %d\en", *ccol_iter_key_ptr(it),
                         *ccol_iter_val_ptr(it));
});
chmap_destroy(freq);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_iter_declare (3),
.BR ccol_begin (3),
.BR ccol_iter_next (3),
.BR ccol_iter_key_ptr (3),
.BR ccol_iter_val_ptr (3)
